</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-A RQ1: Effectiveness on Code Compression ‣ V Results ‣ LongCodeZip: Compress Long Context for Code Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, <a href="#S5.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ V-A RQ1: Effectiveness on Code Compression ‣ V Results ‣ LongCodeZip: Compress Long Context for Code Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, and <a href="#S5.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ V-A RQ1: Effectiveness on Code Compression ‣ V Results ‣ LongCodeZip: Compress Long Context for Code Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> present the evaluation results of our approach on three downstream tasks, respectively. The best scores among compression methods are bolded. Across all three tasks and multiple backbone models, LongCodeZip consistently outperforms compression baselines by substantial and statistically significant margins (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math> via Wilcoxon signed-rank test on 10 repeated experiments), even when operating at comparable or stricter compression ratios.</p>
</div>
